{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target asserts both that the probability of consensus tends to a constant c in (0,1) and that the final number of crossing edges is Theta(sqrt(n)) with high probability. These claims are mutually inconsistent: if H_n = Theta(sqrt(n)) with probability 1-o(1), then P(H_n=0) = o(1), so the probability of the empty matching, i.e. consensus, cannot converge to a positive constant. The Theta(sqrt(n)) prevalence law is stated unconditionally for the final state, leaving no constant-probability mass at H=0.","successor_question":"Is the Theta(sqrt(n)) crossing-edge law meant to hold only conditional on non-consensus? If so, the target needs to state that conditioning explicitly; otherwise the consensus probability cannot be a positive constant.","target":"e68e90124dc50e689db5201795f11db1182571433caa8a63fe4b13bf1c5b6f04"}]}